<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Dream Jobs</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> What Skills Do I Need for a Future Career?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Evaluate arguments in opinion pieces. Reading: Analyze the similarities and differences between two texts.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can evaluate the strength of career predictions in an article, identifying key points and supporting arguments (e.g., "I agree with the argument that there is a growing need for data scientists and software engineers due to technological advancements."). Can compare perspectives in two texts about future careers by highlighting differences in predictions and evidence (e.g., "One article supports remote work due to flexibility, while the other highlights challenges related to productivity."). Can assess arguments in a text about AI's impact on careers (e.g., "I think the article presents a strong case that AI will replace repetitive tasks.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing different futuristic jobs (e.g., AI ethics officer, virtual reality designer, sustainable energy technician, remote healthcare assistant). Students work in pairs to discuss what these jobs entail and what skills they will require. Teacher asks, "What jobs do you foresee in high demand in the next 10-20 years? What new skills will be necessary for these careers?" (Integrates 'jobs,' 'skills,' 'foresee,' 'demand,' 'future').</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher projects a short paragraph from an opinion piece related to future careers. The teacher reads it aloud, demonstrating how to identify the main argument and supporting details. "I will show you how to find the author's main point and the reasons they give to support it. Look for phrases like 'I believe,' 'it is argued that,' or strong predictions using 'will' or 'may.' For instance, 'Artificial intelligence *will transform* industries, creating new opportunities.' This is a prediction, an argument." The teacher highlights key vocabulary like 'transform,' 'industries,' 'opportunities.' (Pronunciation: Teacher models stress in 'artificial intelligence,' 'opportunities').</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is an argument in a text?" (Expected answer: "It's the author's main point or opinion about something.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of words help us identify a prediction about the future?" (Expected answer: "Words like 'will,' 'may,' 'might,' 'foresee,' 'predict,' 'anticipate'.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Dream Jobs"</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Two short opinion pieces (Text A: "The Rise of the Adaptable Workforce: AI and New Opportunities"; Text B: "Navigating the Future: Challenges and Demands of the Evolving Job Market"). Both texts are B1.1 level, featuring target vocabulary (e.g., *automation, remote work, adaptability, innovation, demand, evolve, acquire, technological advancement*) and grammar (future simple, future continuous, modals for possibility).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can understand the main ideas and arguments in opinion pieces about future career trends and the skills required.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students first read Text A silently to get the gist.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main topic of Text A?" "Does the author have a positive or negative view of the future workforce?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read Text B silently.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False questions:</b> Students answer T/F questions based on overall understanding of Text B, e.g., "Text B argues that remote work will increase productivity for all jobs."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific information; evaluating arguments; identifying grammatical structures (future simple, future continuous, modals for possibility); using vocabulary related to future jobs, skills, and technology.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary & Grammar Identification (Controlled Practice):</b> Students reread Text A and highlight all instances of the target vocabulary (e.g., *automation, adaptability, innovation, remote work, technological advancement*) and grammatical structures for prediction (future simple, modals for possibility: *will emerge, may require*). They then complete a cloze exercise with sentences from Text A, filling in missing target vocabulary words or future predictions.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Argument Analysis (Semi-controlled):</b> Students work individually to identify the main arguments presented in Text A. They write down two key arguments and one piece of supporting evidence for each. They then form new pairs to discuss their findings, using phrases like, "I think the article presents a strong case that AI *will replace* repetitive tasks because it mentions the need for *adaptability*." (Pragmatic: Explaining personal career goals indirectly, Sociolinguistic: Group discussion about evolving job markets).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comparing Perspectives (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students now read Text B again, focusing on the arguments. In their pairs, they compare the perspectives presented in Text A and Text B on topics such as AI's impact, remote work, and necessary skills. They use a Venn diagram or T-chart to note similarities and differences. They formulate sentences like, "One article supports *remote work* due to flexibility, while the other highlights challenges related to *productivity*." or "Text A predicts *technological advancement* will create new *opportunities*, whereas Text B focuses on the *disappearance* of traditional *jobs*." (Pronunciation: Teacher guides on stress in 'advancement,' 'adaptability').</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Dream Jobs"</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Future Career Insights Panel"</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In small groups (3-4 students), learners will act as a panel of "career advisors" for high school students. Their task is to synthesize the information from Text A and Text B, evaluate the arguments for and against certain predictions, and present their collective insights on future careers and the essential skills required. Each group will present their main predictions, comparing the perspectives of the two articles and supporting their statements with evidence from the texts. They must use future simple ("Many new jobs *will emerge*"), future continuous ("In ten years, people *will be working* with advanced AI systems"), and modals for possibility ("Jobs *may require* constant *adaptability* and *innovation*"). The focus is on clear communication, critical evaluation of the texts, and collaborative interaction.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example phrases: "We agree with Text A that the *workforce* *will evolve* significantly due to *technological advancement*." "However, Text B makes a strong point that some *jobs* *will disappear*, so we *will be working* on acquiring new *skills*." (Pragmatic: Asking about future career paths in a formal context, Sociolinguistic: Giving advice to an audience with awareness of tone).
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Future Career Insights Panel" presentations, focusing on the group's ability to evaluate arguments, compare texts, and use target language.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Group presentation of "Future Career Insights." Students collectively present their evaluated arguments and comparative analysis from the two articles.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in identifying and evaluating main arguments from Text A and Text B.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective comparison of perspectives in both texts, highlighting similarities and differences.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate and appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., *automation, adaptability, innovation, in-demand, evolve*).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of future simple, future continuous, and modals for predictions and possibilities.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key multisyllabic words and contrastive sounds (e.g., *automation, advancement, adaptability*, /dʒ/ in 'change').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction and coherent group presentation.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences:</p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea about future careers I learned today is..."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found evaluating arguments challenging/easy because..."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy helped me the most today when analyzing the two texts?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I apply what I learned about evaluating arguments to other texts I read?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The students were generally successful in identifying the main arguments in Text A and Text B. The comparison activity required more scaffolding; some groups struggled to articulate nuanced differences. The target vocabulary and grammar structures were integrated well, with students demonstrating understanding, especially of future simple for predictions. Pronunciation of multisyllabic words like 'adaptability' and 'advancement' will need continued practice. For the next lesson, I will provide sentence frames for comparing and contrasting ideas to support those who need more structure.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Today, we focused on reading and evaluating arguments about future careers. In Lesson 3, we will shift to speaking and interaction, where students will actively discuss their own "dream jobs," the skills they are acquiring, and how they anticipate their careers will evolve, building on the information and language from today's reading.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>